# Summary of changes for run 88b1b9d7-e16f-4677-ab33-0cd8b3e90d61
Packaged the Euler–Mascheroni acceleration work into a complete, self-contained publication bundle. All five deliverables are in the project root, committed and pushed.

**ARTICLE.md** (~2,300 words) — a popular-science narrative, "The Half-Step That Squares Your Accuracy". It builds the story from the geometry (the harmonic staircase vs. the hyperbola, and the positive "slivers" between them), states every result inline in natural prose — the midpoint acceleration bound |γ − A₁(n)| ≤ 1/(12(n+1)²) valid for every n ≥ 0, its matching lower bound and sharpness, the envelope transfer theorems, the fourth- and sixth-order accelerations with constants 1/120 and 1/252, the nested enclosure A₁(n) < A₃(n) ≤ γ < A₂(n) of width 1/(12(n+1)²), and the free consequence 1/2 < γ < 7/12 — and closes with the Bernoulli-number structure. All math is properly `$`/`$$` delimited.

**RESEARCH_PAPER.md** (~4,950 words) — abstract, notation, the telescoping identity, the two envelope transfer theorems with full proofs, all five Padé-type inequalities for log(1+x) with their derivative certificates, the second-, fourth- and sixth-order theorems with sharpness, monotonicity and enclosure results, an algorithms-and-numerics section (cost table, a verified numerical table, interval arithmetic), a discussion of where the Bernoulli constants come from, and six open problems.

**RESEARCH_PAPER.tex** — a mirroring LaTeX article (amsmath/amsthm, booktabs, hyperref) with every theorem in a numbered environment, ready for `pdflatex`. (No TeX engine was available in this environment, so it was not compiled here; it uses only standard packages and constructs.)

**demo.py** — self-contained, type-hinted, exact-rational/50-digit numerical demonstration in six parts: error tables against the proved bounds, the sharpness limits, the certified enclosure, the cost of a target accuracy, direct checks of the five logarithm inequalities, and checks of the envelope step inequalities. It runs cleanly and every asserted bound holds.

**PACKAGE.json** — all fields populated: title, domain `Computation`, description, authors, date, six concrete `key_results` in mathematical prose, eight keywords, the full text of the article, both paper formats and the demo, plus
- 2 `demos` (the full numerical laboratory; a script that re-derives B₂, B₄, B₆ and exhaustively verifies every proved inequality for 0 ≤ n ≤ 2000 in 70-digit arithmetic, worst error/bound ratio 0.99999997);
- 3 `algorithms`, each with a professional title, detailed description, formal pseudocode and tested type-hinted code: the certified approximation routine with rational error certificates, the Padé positivity-certificate generator, and an "envelope-to-inequality machine" that converts any envelope into its induced rational inequality and certificate (it independently reproduces the contact orders x⁴, x³, x⁶, x⁵, x⁸ quoted in the paper);
- 3 `visualizations` (order-of-convergence log–log plot; sliver geometry with envelope tracking; sharpness and nested enclosure);
- 2 `interactive_demos` (a slider-driven Acceleration Explorer with live error/bound tightness bars, enclosure strip and log–log canvas; an interactive sliver-and-envelope dissection with a live envelope-condition table);
- an `interactive_layout` written as a ten-section guided notebook narrative that embeds every element by placeholder and hides proofs and background behind styled `<details>` sections;
- `lean_proofs` and `lean_files` carrying the complete formal source and its three paths through from the previous phase, and the future directions.

No prose deliverable mentions any implementation or verification machinery; each stands entirely on its own.
